PAYS DOWN CARRIED-FORWARD DEBT
Phase 2's note recorded that lib/tax/classification.ts was not yet consumed
by any schedule builder and that Phase 4 must route through it rather than
re-deriving. This does. Three tests assert the NEGATIVE cases explicitly -
an inherited capital_improvement and an inherited initial_repair must not
appear on the repairs line, and a manual override is honoured in both
directions - so the vocabulary defect cannot reappear through this path.

ORDERING DECISION
Cash expenses alphabetically (body corporate, council rates, insurance,
interest, land tax, agent fees, repairs, water, sundry), then the two
computed deductions (capital works Div 43, decline in value Div 40) last.
Computed deductions have no invoice behind them, so grouping them apart
from paid expenses makes the evidence index legible. Empty lines are
omitted rather than shown as zero.

OTHER BEHAVIOUR
- primary_residence returns an excluded result with a reason, not an empty
  schedule that could be mistaken for a property with no activity.
- Renovation repairs and operating repairs_maintenance merge onto ONE
  repairs line; two separate lines would not match a rental schedule.
- Non-claimable renovations excluded.
- declineInValue omitted entirely when no QS figure exists, rather than
  shown as zero - zero would imply a determination was made.
- Apportionment applied per line via apportionIncome/apportionDeduction,
  preserving the income-vs-deduction asymmetry from Phase 3.

